nternational Conference on Computational Linguistics, Language Resources and Evaluation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-5e6d8889-8e32-4cb4-a124-5b106698df8a","itu-5671851b-0bf7-4b68-86b0-0e1120971c10"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Can Humans Identify Domains?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-094.66-741398","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Language from social media remains challenging to process automatically, especially for non-English languages. In this work, we introduce the first NLP dataset for TikTok comments and the first Danish social media dataset with part-of-speech annotation. We further supply annotations for normalization, code-switching, and annotator uncertainty. As transferring models to such a highly specialized domain is non-trivial, we conduct an extensive study into which source data and modeling decisions most impact the performance. Surprisingly, transferring from in-domain data, even from a different language, outperforms in-language, out-of-domain training. These benefits nonetheless rely on the underlying language models having been at least partially pre-trained on data from the target language. Using our additional annotation layers, we further analyze how normalization, code-switching, and human uncertainty affect the tagging accuracy.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Hansen, Kia Kirstein","Barrett, Maria Jung","Müller-Eberstein, Max","Damgaard, Cathrine","Eriksen, Trine","van der Goot, Rob"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695305"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Code switching","Language model transfer","NLP dataset","Part of speech annotation","Social media text"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dantok domain beats language for danish social media pos tagging","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.nodalida-1.27.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695305","identifier":[{"id":"85217079230","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e0df3bd5-afde-4e7a-a53e-61966056a502"],"keyword":["Code-switching","Language model transfer","NLP dataset","Part-of-speech annotation","Social media text"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-544A23353BEA20FC514659AF261A7675"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.nodalida-1.27.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Kia Kirstein","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Kia Kirstein","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria Jung","key":"barrettpau","last":"Barrett","locid":"ffc6db77-95ed-452f-92ac-ca143a9db73c","name":"Barrett, Maria Jung","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Max","key":"müllerebersteinpau","last":"Müller-Eberstein","locid":"523ffd96-f3b2-49cc-8f24-dc9215b84bed","name":"Müller-Eberstein, Max","orcid":"0000-0002-0006-0658","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Cathrine","key":"damgaardpau","last":"Damgaard","name":"Damgaard, Cathrine","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","name":"Eriksen, Trine","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rob","key":"vandergootpau","last":"van der Goot","locid":"8e5f2a6c-c817-4a90-b48b-d9171743ad0f","name":"van der Goot, Rob","orcid":"0009-0003-1999-4156","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"271-279","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 24th Nordic Conference on Computational Linguistics (NoDaLiDa)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e0df3bd5-afde-4e7a-a53e-61966056a502"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"DanTok: Domain Beats Language for Danish Social Media POS Tagging","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695305","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Cities can host significant biological diversity. Yet, urbanisation leads to the loss of habitats, species, and functional groups. Understanding how multiple taxa respond to urbanisation globally is essential to promote and conserve biodiversity in cities. Using a dataset encompassing six terrestrial faunal taxa (amphibians, bats, bees, birds, carabid beetles and reptiles) across 379 cities on 6 continents, we show that urbanisation produces taxon-specific changes in trait composition, with traits related to reproductive strategy showing the strongest response. Our findings suggest that urbanisation results in four trait syndromes (mobile generalists, site specialists, central place foragers, and mobile specialists), with resources associated with reproduction and diet likely driving patterns in traits associated with mobility and body size. Functional diversity measures showed varied responses, leading to shifts in trait space likely driven by critical resource distribution and abundance, and taxon-specific trait syndromes. Maximising opportunities to support taxa with different urban trait syndromes should be pivotal in conservation and management programmes within and among cities. This will reduce the likelihood of biotic homogenisation and helps ensure that urban environments have the capacity to respond to future challenges. These actions are critical to reframe the role of cities in global biodiversity loss.","author_count":59,"contributors":["Hahs, Amy K.","Fournier, Bertrand","Aronson, Myla F.J.","Nilon, Charles H.","Herrera-Montes, Adriana","Salisbury, Allyson B.","Threlfall, Caragh G.","Rega-Brodsky, Christine C.","Lepczyk, Christopher A.","La Sorte, Frank A.","MacGregor-Fors, Ian","Scott MacIvor, J.","Jung, Kirsten","Piana, Max R.","Williams, Nicholas S.G.","Knapp, Sonja","Vergnes, Alan","Acevedo, Aldemar A.","Gainsbury, Alison M.","Rainho, Ana","Hamer, Andrew J.","Shwartz, Assaf","Voigt, Christian C.","Lewanzik, Daniel","Lowenstein, David M.","O’Brien, David","Tommasi, Desiree","Pineda, Eduardo","Carpenter, Ela Sita","Belskaya, Elena","Lövei, Gabor L","Makinson, James C.","Coleman, Joanna L.","Sadler, Jon P.","Shroyer, Jordan","Shapiro, Julie Teresa","Baldock, Katherine C.R.","Ksiazek-Mikenas, Kelly","Matteson, Kevin C.","Barrett, Kyle","Siles, Lizette","Aguirre, Luis F.","Armesto, Luis Orlando","Zalewski, Marcin","Herrera-Montes, Maria Isabel","Obrist, Martin K.","Tonietto, Rebecca K.","Gagné, Sara A.","Hinners, Sarah J.","Latty, Tanya","Surasinghe, Thilina D.","Sattler, Thomas","Magura, Tibor","Ulrich, Werner","Elek, Zoltan","Castañeda-Oviedo, Jennifer","Torrado, Ricardo","Kotze, D. Johan","Moretti, Marco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-688112"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, Central","America, North","America, South","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Fournier, Bertrand","Hahs, Amy K.","Kotze, D. Johan","Moretti, Marco"],"country":["Australia","Bolivia","Canada","Chile","Colombia","Finland","France","Germany","Hungary","Israel","Mexico","Poland","Portugal","Puerto Rico","Russia","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["AC Red Biol Evolut, Instituto Nacional de Ecologia - Mexico, Inst Ecol","All Global Affiliations","Auburn University","Bridgewater State University","City University of New York","Clemson University","ETH Zürich","Elmhurst College","German Centre for Integrative Biodiversity Research (iDiv)","Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","Hungarian Academy of Sciences","Leibniz Institute for Zoo and Wildlife Research","Macquarie University","Michigan State University","Morton Arboretum","NatureScot","Nicolaus Copernicus University in Torun","Northumbria University","Pittsburg State University","Polish Academy of Sciences","RAS - Ural Branch","Rutgers - The State University of New Jersey, New Brunswick","Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","Swiss Ornithological Institute","Technical University of Berlin","Technion-Israel Institute of Technology","US Dept Agriculture","Ulm University","United States Fish & Wildlife Service","Universidad Austral de Chile","Universidad Mayor de San Simon","Universidad de Pamplona","Universidad del Valle","Universite Claude Bernard Lyon 1","Universite de Montpellier","University of Birmingham","University of Bristol","University of California at Santa Cruz","University of Debrecen","University of Helsinki","University of Lisbon","University of Melbourne","University of Miami","University of Michigan","University of Missouri","University of North Carolina","University of Potsdam","University of Puerto Rico","University of South Florida","University of Sydney","University of Utah","Weill Cornell Medical College","Western Sydney University"],"keyword":["Animals","Bees","Biodiversity","Birds","Chiroptera","Ecosystem","Syndrome","Urbanization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urbanisation generates multiple trait syndromes for terrestrial animal taxa worldwide","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-39746-1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-688112","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10406945","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85166786070","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37550318","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-aafa28c6-ac25-4ef0-8776-39f3f2abd8e7"],"keyword":["Animals","Bees","Biodiversity","Birds","Chiroptera","Ecosystem","Syndrome","Urbanization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-999D522CF1FE04AEEE3F9B928F2DF1C0"],"oa_link":[{